{% if vpnc_net_join_server %}
{{ inventory_hostname }} is the vpnc-net server ({{ vpnc_net_ip }}): ansible-playbook monitoring.yml sets it up.
{% else %}
{{ inventory_hostname }} ({{ vpnc_net_ip }}) is in vpnc-net. Its compose file:

  {{ vpnc_net_join_compose }}

It is private: VPNC_JOIN holds the probe's key. {{ 'The tunnel goes through the service links (outbound_access_policy: vpn).' if vpnc_net_via else 'The tunnel goes straight to ' ~ vpnc_net_endpoint ~ ' over UDP (outbound_access_policy: ' ~ outbound_access_policy ~ '). If the network blocks it, set outbound_access_policy: vpn for the probe in the inventory and put links into files/probes/' ~ inventory_hostname ~ '/service_links.txt. Then, with SSH to its host, run probes-host.yml --limit ' ~ inventory_hostname ~ '; without it, run this again and start the new compose.yml there with an empty ./state: the token is read only into an empty one.' }}

1. On the machine (Linux or macOS with Docker), in a folder of its own:
     docker compose up -d
   Then `tail -f logs/aio.log` there: "wg INFO handshake_ok" means it is in.
2. Here: put the links to check into files/probes/{{ inventory_hostname }}/links.txt, then
     ansible-playbook probes.yml --limit {{ inventory_hostname }}
3. Delete compose.yml here once it runs there; the machine keeps its own copy.
{% endif %}
